🚀 The feature, motivation and pitch
GetIrValueForScalarFromCodegen is used by codegen.



      pytorch/torch/csrc/lazy/core/lazy_graph_executor.cpp


        Lines 532 to 543
      in
      7bf1b1d







           Value LazyGraphExecutor::GetIrValueForScalarFromCodegen( 




               const at::Scalar& value) { 




             if (IsSpecialScalar(value)) { 




               return MakeScalar(value, value.type()); 




             } 




             auto cpu_device = getBackend()->GetBackendDevice(c10::Device(c10::kCPU, 0)); 




             BackendDataPtr data = 




                 getBackend()->MakeComputationDataFromScalar(value, cpu_device); 




             data->SetInfo( 




                 std::make_shared<DeviceDataInfo>(/*tensor_id=*/-1, /*read_only=*/true)); 




             return MakeDeviceData(std::move(data)); 




           } 





LTC upload the scalar to the CPU device and make a Device data out of it. This will create segfault on PyTorch/XLA since we expect all Value to be on the same device. In fact we don't support upload to the at::CPU since it is not a XLA device.
I have think of a couple ways to handle this

upload to the default device instead. Backend currently sets the default device in 


      pytorch/torch/csrc/lazy/backend/backend_interface.h


         Line 101
      in
      7bf1b1d







           virtual std::shared_ptr<BackendDeviceType> GetDefaultDeviceType() const = 0; 





In the op that takes scalar, it must also takes some other at::Tensor that is on a lazy device. We just need to pick one at::Tensor and upload the scalar to the same device. This is the approach is what PyTorch/XLA takes today which should be the most correct thing to do.
Don't wrap Scalar into IR. I tried to reuse TreatScalarsAsConstants that already existed, but I don't think I can use this option in the full_codegen section

@Krovatkin @wconstab
Alternatives
XLA don't codegen ops that takes scalar which is not ideal.
